# Project 2 — IAM Design

## Overview
This project defines a secure Identity and Access Management (IAM) architecture for AI agents and services.

## IAM Architecture
The design follows least privilege, separation of duties, strong authentication, and controlled access.

## Identity Lifecycle
- Provision unique identities for each AI agent.
- Assign only required permissions.
- Review access regularly.
- Rotate credentials periodically.
- Immediately revoke compromised or unused identities.
- Decommission identities when agents are retired.

## Authentication
- Use strong authentication mechanisms.
- Avoid shared credentials.
- Use short-lived tokens wherever possible.
- Protect API keys and secrets using secure secret management.

## Authorization
- Apply role-based access control.
- Follow the principle of least privilege.
- Restrict agents to only the resources required for their tasks.
- Separate administrative and operational permissions.

## Credential Governance
- Rotate API keys and secrets regularly.
- Do not store credentials in source code.
- Monitor credential usage.
- Revoke exposed or compromised credentials immediately.

## Security Controls
- Least privilege access
- Unique agent identities
- Credential rotation
- Access reviews
- Secret management
- Audit logging
- Immediate revocation

## Conclusion
The IAM design provides controlled, auditable, and secure access for AI agents while reducing the risk of credential misuse and unauthorized access.
